        "b1_96022": {
          "type": "text",
          "name": "t:names.heading",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>Walk into any pharmacy or health store in Kuwait and the supplement shelf tells you everything. Bright tubs designed for a different market. Arabic translations stuck on as adhesive labels over the original English. Formulations designed for Western consumers and shipped east with a translated sticker and a Halal logo added at the last stage.</p><p>The brands are not bad. Many of them are good products in their home markets. But they were not built for women in the Gulf. They were not formulated with this region’s dietary habits, cultural expectations, or daily routines in mind. They were adapted, not designed. And adapted is not the same thing.</p><p>We looked at that shelf and saw a gap that no Western brand was going to close, because closing it requires starting from this side of the world, not arriving from the other.</p>",
            "highlight_style": "none",
            "highlight_font_style": "italic",
            "highlight_shape_color": "",
            "highlight_text_color": "",
            "type_preset": "rte",
            "font": "var(--font-heading--family)",
            "font_weight": "",
            "font_size": 58,
            "font_size_mobile": 40,
